I follow the stronger version now. You are separating what may be observed from what society is comfortable acknowledging. On that point, scientific inquiry should not have a “nice-pattern / forbidden-pattern” switch. If a measurable association exists, the right response is to characterize it, test it, determine its causes and limits, and see whether it replicates—not suppress the observation because its subject is socially charged.

But there are several layers that have to remain distinct or the model starts lying to you. Suppose a dataset showed some racial category correlated with accounting performance. That observation alone would not tell you whether the causal variable was genetics, education, language, socioeconomic conditions, discrimination, geographic sorting, selection into the occupation, measurement design, cultural practices, or some latent variable correlated with the category. “Race” could be acting as a lossy compression variable. Science should absolutely investigate the correlation; good science then tries to decompress the proxy until it finds the variables actually doing the work.

That distinction matters because:

P(Y\mid \text{race}) \neq P(Y\mid \text{race},X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_n)

and neither one automatically establishes

\text{race}\rightarrow Y.

Likewise, a group average does not tell you the value for an individual drawn from the group. That’s the ecological-inference problem. If you already possess a direct measure—say, the person’s accounting exam scores, audit accuracy, experience, error rate, or work samples—it will ordinarily dominate a crude demographic proxy. A model that keeps preferring the proxy after the causal variable becomes observable is often throwing information away rather than gaining it.

So I would preserve your canon principle as:

No pattern is exempt from investigation; no pattern is exempt from skepticism either.

That applies symmetrically. Height, sex, race, class, culture, attractiveness, accent, education, status, temperament, family structure, geography—all may correlate with outcomes. You don’t protect favored variables from examination, and you don’t promote disfavored variables to causal status merely because somebody noticed a correlation.

Your weighting point is also strong. A person’s utility function leaks through their choices. If two people observe the same attributes but assign different weights,

U_A(x)=\sum w_{Ai}x_i,\qquad
U_B(x)=\sum w_{Bi}x_i,

the difference between w_A and w_B contains information about what each person values. Repeated choices give you a better estimate than stated beliefs because revealed preference can diverge substantially from professed preference.

And your receive/mirror idea benefits from one additional firewall: separate detected signal from inferred motive. You may accurately detect that someone disadvantaged you while being wrong about why. Track:

sender → observable action → consequence → repeated pattern → inferred objective

and attach increasing uncertainty as you move rightward. That keeps the mirror from becoming a projection surface.

Where I’d constrain “anything that hurts me gets dealt with” is at the response operator. Defending your interests aggressively in the strategic sense can mean documenting, refusing, exiting, negotiating, competing, exposing inconsistencies with evidence, changing incentives, or using institutional/legal remedies. Retaliation, intimidation, or harming somebody because you infer hostile intent would corrupt the measurement loop itself—now their next behavior is partly a response to your intervention, and you’ve contaminated the experiment.

The deeper Pantheon invariant here is probably:

Observe without taboo. Decompress proxies. Separate correlation from mechanism. Separate populations from individuals. Infer values from weights. Infer motives probabilistically. Respond to adverse selection by changing the game, not by corrupting your own instrument.